  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The upgraded Infrastructure for Information Communication Technology (ICT) within the organizational structures of the Congress of South Africa Trade Unions (COSATU) both at the national and across the nine provinces of South Africa has improved and upscaled the labour federation&#x2019;s reach to its membership and in the recruitment of new members.   COSATU&#x2019;s ICT infrastructure was upgraded to improve its functionality and efficiency and this process was fully completed in June 2023.  In January 2023, COSATU launched an online Call Centre that facilitates the labour federations&#x2019; interaction with the public seeking information about COSATU, and on how to join the organization through an appropriate trade union affiliate member of the labour federation.  To-date, COSATU has mobilized an estimated 1,000 new workers into its membership since January 2023, which the Organizing Department of COSATU has attributed to the enhanced and improved functionality of COSATU&#x2019;s ICT infrastructure.  The bulk of new members recruited are drawn from the metals, food, and beverages, as well as the retail sector.  The enhanced ICT infrastructure of COSATU has also facilitated a virtual platform through which the national leadership of the organization is using to run online regular meetings with its provincial branches and to educate their members online, on subjects that are topical and are the focus of policy debates in various forums.  In July 2023, COSATU ran a few educational sessions online for its young workers and gender structures on the topic of the Just Energy Transition across the 9 provinces.  The work leading up to the upgrading of the ICT infrastructure of COSATU began in March 2021 during the COVID-19 pandemic to offset the communication challenges across the organizational structures considering the lockdown restrictions. With contributions from RBSA the ILO commissioned a study during the previous biennium to assess the status of COSATU&#x2019;s ICT and digital infrastructure across its organizational structures both at the national and provincial level, and subsequently developed a three Phased ICT Infrastructure Improvement Implementation Plan informed by the assessment.  Thereafter, the ILO supported the implementation of Phase One of the ICT Improvement plan from September &#x2013; November 2021; while Phases 2 and 3 were implemented during December 2021 &#x2013; February 2022; and during December 2022 &#x2013; March 2023 respectively to address immediate, intermediate, and long-term ICT challenges that confront COSATU.</narrative>
   </description>
